wolf build — compile an entry file to a native executable.

usage: wolf build <file.lu> [-o OUT] [--emit=wir|obj|bin|llvm-ir] [--no-cache]
            [--verbose] [--checked] [-g] [--release] [--codegen-report]
            [-q|--quiet] [--timings|--timings=json:<file>]
            [--profile-gen[=<dir>]] [--profile=<file.wprof>] [--std-root <dir>]
            [--allow|--warn|--deny <W####|W##xx|warnings>] [--deny-warnings]
            [--error-limit=N] [--target x86_64-unknown-none] [--assembler PATH] [--linker PATH]

The entry file's directory is the package (D32): every plain `.lu` file
beside it is part of the same module. Two tiers compile the same source
— the default is the compiler's own fast backend, and `--release` goes
through LLVM. A construct the pipeline cannot yet handle is REFUSED by
name, with the deepest phase that completed; nothing is silently
interpreted instead.

Native debug builds include source types and whole-function variable slots.
`-g` additionally emits nonallocated `.debug_frame` information for debugger
and profiler stack walks. Frame pointers remain enabled. This is not runtime
unwinding: no loaded unwind tables, personality routines or landing pads are
introduced. Unknown teardown forms are reported as CFI_GAP and left uncovered
rather than described with a wrong rule. The release tier does not yet emit
source debug information and refuses `-g`.

`--assembler PATH` (or `--assembler=PATH`) overrides `WOLF_AS` for
root-manifest assembly sources. The selected program receives `--64`
(on x86-64), `-o OUTPUT SOURCE`; it is a path, not a shell command.
Failure is final. Without either choice, assembly uses the existing
CC/cc (hosted) or WOLF_CLANG/clang (freestanding) driver. This does not
replace the linker or the release backend's clang IR compilation, and
it does not enable hosted Windows assembly or foreign hosted targets.
`--linker PATH` (or `--linker=PATH`) overrides nonempty `WOLF_LD`
for native Linux ELF links. The selected tool receives the cc-shaped
link line: `-o OUTPUT OBJECTS RUNTIME -lpthread -ldl -lm
-Wl,--gc-sections` and trap aliases when needed. It is one executable
path, not shell text or a raw ld command. Failure or malformed/foreign
ELF output is final. Without either choice, the existing CC/cc, macOS
and Windows link paths are unchanged. Explicit linkers do not enable
hosted Windows, Mach-O or foreign targets, and are unused by object-only
builds. Assembly and release clang IR compilation remain separate.
For `wolf run`, put build flags before the entry file.

A terminal gets a live unit/phase line; other stderr stays silent until
30 seconds without output, then heartbeats every 60 seconds. TERM=dumb
uses heartbeats, never cursor controls. WOLF_STATUS_AFTER=<seconds>
overrides the first deadline (0..86400, decimals accepted; invalid uses
30). `-q`/`--quiet` hides the reporter, never diagnostics. `--timings`
prints scoped elapsed contributions on stderr; `--timings=json:<file>`
writes wolf-build-timings/1 JSON (even under quiet). See docs/cli.md for
the schema and overlapping parallel/nested scopes.

`--target x86_64-unknown-none` (or `target: "x86_64-unknown-none"` in
`wolf.pkg`) builds a freestanding object for a kernel: `--emit=obj`
only, no `main` shim and no runtime library; the entry is an `export
fn`, a trap calls the program's `wolf_trap`, and anything that needs the
hosted runtime, allocates, or uses a float is refused by name.

